5:[["$","$L10","3.10",{"topic":{"id":"3.10","title":"Mobile Forensics","minutes":14,"summary":"Handle phones as evolving, encrypted, cloud-connected systems whose extraction labels do not guarantee completeness.","sections":[{"title":"Device state and preservation","body":["Record model, identifiers, lock state, connectivity, battery, operating system, and visible time. Determine whether the device is unlocked, after first unlock, or fully locked, because these states can affect accessible data. Remote wipe and network synchronization are risks, but isolation can also change evidence and device behavior.","There is no universal airplane-mode instruction. RF isolation, power management, and supported preservation methods require a tested procedure and qualified judgment. Keep the device powered where appropriate, document actions, and avoid repeated unlock attempts. Obtain the necessary authority and consent or legal process for access."]},{"title":"Extraction methods and application data","body":["Manual review, logical export, filesystem extraction, backup-based methods, and exploit-assisted techniques differ in scope and risk. A tool’s “full” extraction label may omit app data protected by separate encryption or unsupported formats. Record the exact tool, version, method, device state, and what could not be accessed.","Messages may exist in application databases, cloud backups, paired devices, and provider records. Deleted-message recovery depends on database behavior, retention, encryption, and overwriting. Preserve original databases and decode timestamps carefully. Screenshots can supplement but should not replace a reliable export when one is lawfully available."]},{"title":"Location, health, and attribution","body":["Location records can originate from GPS, Wi-Fi, cellular observations, photographs, or applications. Each has different accuracy and semantics. A point on a map may represent an estimated area or a cached location, not a person’s exact movement. Health and wearable records may be synchronized and transformed by the companion app.","A phone in someone’s possession is not conclusive proof that they authored every message. Consider shared access, remote compromise, notifications, and scheduled actions. Corroborate important events with device logs, provider records, and independent context. Minimize exposure of unrelated intimate data during examination and disclosure."]}],"practice":{"title":"Choose a mobile preservation plan","scenario":"A complainant offers an unlocked phone containing a relevant chat and consents to a scoped export.","steps":["Record device state and the consent or authority boundary.","Compare a supported chat export with broader extraction options.","Preserve the original export, identifiers, and relevant context.","List cloud and recipient records that may require separate requests."],"debrief":"A narrow supported export may be sufficient and proportionate. More invasive extraction is not automatically more reliable or lawful."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss","dpdp"],"sources":["cert","privacy","bsa"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain device state and preservation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain extraction methods and application data and apply it to an investigation.","Explain location, health, and attribution and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.10","prompt":"What should an examiner record about a mobile extraction?","options":["Only the phone’s colour.","The method, tool version, device state, scope, and limitations.","Only whether the screen was on.","A guarantee that every deleted message was recovered."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extraction completeness depends on the device, method, access state, and application support.","unit":3}}}]]
